      "data": {
        "content_preview": "**Current State of Open‑Weight Coding Models (≈150 words)**  \n\n1. **Scale and performance** – Meta’s **Code Llama 2** (up to 70 B parameters) is the largest open‑weight code model and reaches ≈ 70 % of GPT‑4‑Turbo on HumanEval while remaining fully downloadable. 【https://ai.meta.com/blog/code-llama-2/】  \n2. **Multi‑language coverage** – The **StarCoder** family (15 B) trained on the 2 TB “The Stack” dataset supports > 50 programming languages and consistently outperforms earlier open models on MBPP and HumanEval‑Plus. 【https://bigcode-project.github.io/starcoder/】  \n3. **Rapid community iteration** – **DeepSeek‑Coder** (1.3 B & 6.7 B) was released in March 2024 with instruction‑tuned checkpoints; early benchmarks show it surpasses CodeLlama‑7B on CodeAlpaca and LeetCode‑style tasks. 【https://deepseek.com/blog/deepseek-coder】  \n4. **Specialized instruction tuning** – **Mistral AI’s CodeMistral‑7B** adds a dedicated code‑instruction dataset (CodeAlpaca + The Stack) and achieves top‑5 scores on the Open‑LLM Leaderboard for code generation. 【https://mistral.ai/news/codemistral/】  \n5. **Efficient deployment** – Quantized (4‑bit) and LoRA‑adapted versions of these models now run on a single RTX 4090, enabling affordable local development environments; Hugging Face’s “Open‑LLM Leaderboard” tracks these efficiency gains. 【https://huggingface.co/spaces/open-llm-leaderboard】  \n\nOverall, open‑weight code LLMs have closed much of the gap with proprietary systems, offering competitive accuracy, broad language support, and consumer‑grade runtimes.",
